  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe marine layer deepened much more this morning with the passing of \nhigh clouds pushing to the east. Clouds will continue to erode \nnear the coast, though there may be only intermittent clearing at \nthe beaches into the afternoon, especially in San Diego County. A \ntrough offshore will keep temperatures below normal again today \ninto the weekend. Highs will remain in the 80s and 90s for most \nvalley, foothill, and high desert locations during this time \nperiod. High pressure to the east and the trough to the west will \nhelp funnel in some monsoonal moisture through the weekend as \nwell, making it feel more on the humid side. Lack of instability \nand drier southwest flow aloft remains the biggest proponent of \nany widespread storm activity, so rain chances remain small across\nthe mountains and adjacent deserts. Moisture decreases slightly \nby Saturday, moreso into Sunday. If any rain falls over the next \ncouple of days, it will be light and localized.\n\nHigh pressure begins to expand by next week. By Monday,\ntemperatures will warm to near seasonal averages. The high \nexpands a bit further into the middle of next week, where \ntemperatures will peak around 5 degrees above average. This trend \nwill also lead to drier and lower humidity across the board. The \nmarine layer is expected to remain in a healthy and shallower \nstate next week as well, covering coastal areas and far western \nvalleys with clouds and patchy fog each night and morning.",
